----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Installing OpenParEM using the package with pre-compiled binaries
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The pre-compiled binaries allow OpenParEM to be run on x86 Linux platforms without
installing any additional packages. The installation process is to download and 
unpack the binary distribution, set the environment variables, then run the 
test script to verify successful operation.

Execution with the pre-compiled binary is validated for the following operating
systems:

Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS
Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS
Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS
Debian 13 (trixie)
Fedora 44 (Workstation Edition)
openSUSE 16.0 (Leap)
Windows WSL2 running Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS

Detailed instructions:

1. Download the installation package OpenParEM-version-bin.tar.gz.
2. Copy the installation package to an installation directory "install_dir".
3. cd to install_dir and unpack the installation directory using the commands:

   $ tar -xf OpenParEM-version-bin.tar.gz
   $ ln -s OpenParEM-version OpenParEM

   Setting the link enables new revisions to be installed without having to
   modifiy the environment variables in the shell configuration file.

4. In directory $HOME, edit the configuration file for the shell used for
   command line processing.

   example:
      a. cd $HOME
      b. check which shell is being used
         $ echo $SHELL
         /bin/bash
      c. for bash, the configuration file is .bashrc
      d. edit .bashrc with any text editor

5. Set the following environment variables in the shell configuration file.

   export PATH=install_dir/OpenParEM/bin:install_dir/OpenParEM/scripts:$PATH
   export FONTCONFIG_PATH=install_dir/OpenParEM/etc/fonts
   export OPAL_PREFIX=install_dir/OpenParEM
   export PMIX_PREFIX=install_dir/OpenParEM

   After editing and saving the configuration file, either source the modified
   version or exit and start a new terminal to pick up the changes.

6. Copy the FreeCAD and ParaView Python scripts to the default locations.

   $ cd install_dir/OpenParEM/scripts
   $ OpenParEM_build --without-all

7. Test the installation:

   $ cd install_dir/OpenParEM/scripts
   $ OpenParEM_test

   If the two tests pass, then the installation is complete.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Installing OpenParEM by Compiling from Source
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

OpenParEM can be compiled from source, which is the preferred method for using
OpenParEM if the necessary build tools are installed or can be installed on the
system.

The installation process is to download and unpack the source distribution or
clone the project from GitHub, run the compilation script, install any missing
compilation tools, re-run if needed, then update the PATH envinroment variable.

Compilation is validated for the following x86 Linux operating systems:

Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS
Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS
Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS
Debian 13 (trixie)
openSUSE 16.0 (Leap)
Windows WSL2 running Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS

Compililation completes but fails validation for the following operating systems:
Fedora 44 (Workstation Edition)

--------------------------
Required Compilation Tools
--------------------------

A number of tools must be installed on the system for compilation to complete,
such as make, g++, gfortran, etc.  The build script OpenParEM_build checks for
the presence of the needed tools and reports any missing.  If all tools are found,
then compilation proceeds.

Install any needed tools with the package installation manager on the Linux
system.  Note that this requires sudo administrator priviledges.

----------------------------
Compilation and Installation 
----------------------------

1. Install source code by either installing the installation package or cloning
   from GitHub.

   1.1. Installing using the installation package
      1.1.1. Download the installation package OpenParEM-version.tar.gz.
      1.1.2. Copy the installation package to a src directory "src_dir"
      1.1.3. cd to src_dir and unpack the src tree using the commands:

             $ tar -xf OpenParEM-version.tar.gz
             $ ln -s OpenParEM-version OpenParEM

             Setting the link enables new revisions to be installed without having to
             modifiy the PATH environment variable in the shell configuration file.

   1.2. Installing by cloning from GitHub
      1.2.1. Install git on the system if not already installed.
      1.2.2. Clone from GitHub using the following command:

             $ git clone https://github.com/OpenParEM/OpenParEM 

4. cd to OpenParEM/scripts.
5. Compile and install in the src tree by running the script "OpenParEM_build".

   example:
      $ ./OpenParEM_build --jobs=4

      The installation directory install_dir=src_dir/OpenParEM and the
      compilers use 4 processors for faster compilation.  Generally, set jobs to
      the number of high-performance cores on the system.

   Compilation does not require administrator priviledges unless the --prefix
   option below is specified with a location in an area where the user does not
   have write priviledges.

   Some compiling issues are reported with a message "See 'config.log' for
   more details".  The config.log file is located in the directory where
   the error occured, not the directory from which the OpenParEM_build is run.
   To find the config.log file, execute

      $ find .. -name "config.log" -print

6. OpenParEM can be in installed in a different location given as "install_dir"
   by providing the location to the build script with the --prefix option.

   example installing in the directory $HOME/local:
      $ OpenParEM_build --jobs=4 --prefix=$HOME/local

      In this case, install_dir=$HOME/local.  The directory "local" must
      exist before running the script.

7. When compilation completes, OpenParEM2D and OpenParEM3D are tested. If
   the two tests pass, then the basic installation is complete and just the
   PATH environment variable needs to be set.

8. In directory $HOME, edit the configuration file for the shell used for
   command line processing.

   example:
      a. cd $HOME
      b. check which shell is being used
         $ echo $SHELL
         /bin/bash
      c. for bash, the configuration file is .bashrc
      d. edit .bashrc with any text editor

9. Set the following environment variables in the shell configuration file.

   export PATH=install_dir/OpenParEM/bin:install_dir/OpenParEM/scripts:$PATH

   After editing and saving the configuration file, either source the modified
   version or exit and start a new terminal to pick up the changes.

10. In the output of the build script, look for the tests of OpenParEM2D and
    OpenParEM3D.  For a successful compilation, the two should be reported with
    "pass".  Scroll back up the output to find the messages.
11. To check that the various paths have been set properly, the execution tests
    can be re-run. Test the installation with these commands:

   $ cd src_dir/OpenParEM/scripts
   $ ./OpenParEM_test

   If the two tests pass, then the installation is verified.

------------
Compile Time
------------

1. Compile time depends on the system and the number of jobs.  For a typical
   desktop PC or workstation with jobs set to the number of physical cores,
   expect approximately 1 hour for compiling.
2. Almost all of the compile time is spent on libraries such as OpenMPI, OCCT,
   and Qt.  The OpenParEM code itself only requires a few minutes.
3. By compiling all required libraries except basic OS libraries like libc and
   libm, library compatibility and version issues are avoided, plus the pre-
   compiled library requires no packages to be installed, so the user does not
   require root priviliges to run sudo [or having to get a sys admin to install
   packages].

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OpenMPI and MPICH
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

OpenMPI is used in the pre-compiled binaries and is the default in the build
script.  MPICH can used instead of OpenMPI by setting the command line switch
--with-mpich to the build script to compile with MPICH.

   example:
      $ OpenParEM_build --jobs=4 --with-mpich

Note that all development, testing, and validation is done with OpenMPI.  Should
problems occur when running with MPICH, please contact the developers.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Startup Warnings
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A harmless warning can appear on some systems:

qt.qpa.services: Failed to register with host portal 
QDBusError("org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.ServiceUnknown", 
"The name org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop was not provided by any .service files")

If desired, this warning can be eliminated by installing package 
"xdg-desktop-portal".  For example, on Ubuntu the command is
   $ sudo apt-get install xdg-desktop-portal

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Uninstalling OpenParEM
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. Remove scripts and helper files:
   $ cd install_dir/OpenParEM/scripts
   $ OpenParEM_build --remove-helper-scripts
2. Remove OpenParEM:
   $ cd install_dir
   $ rm -Rf OpenParEM
3. Remove the configuration file for OpenParEMg:
   $ cd $HOME
   $ rm -f .OpenParEMg
4. In directory $HOME, edit the configuration file for the shell used for
   command line processing and remove all previous entries made referring to
   OpenParEM.

